<p>
I do a <i>lot</i> of writing, almost all of it in Emacs so I&rsquo;m always happy to see how other people use our editor of choice for writing. The latest example I&rsquo;ve found is from JTR over at <a href="https://taonaw.com">The Art Of Not Asking Why</a>. His post considers some <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/08/29/emacs-config-gems-part.html#fnref:6">simple expansion functions in Emacs</a>.
</p>
<p>
He starts off with dictionaries. As many of you know, I&rsquo;ve been a Webster 1913 dictionary user ever since <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=4190">James Somers explained why I (and you) should be</a>. I have gone through many iterations of integrating it into Emacs but have converged on the same solution that JTR uses: the built in <code>dictionary-search</code> function built into Emacs that we both have configured to use the online collection <code>dict.org</code>, although I still have a local copy from my previous iterations.
</p>
<p>
The <code>dict.org</code> collection is nice because it contains several dictionaries besides Webster&rsquo;s, and <code>dictionary-search</code> will return entries from all of them that have the word you&rsquo;re looking for. The best part is that it&rsquo;s trivial to set up You don&rsquo;t have to download dictionaries or packages. You simply set a variable saying you want to use <code>dict.org</code> and that&rsquo;s it. There <i>are</i> some nuances with that so be sure to read JTR&rsquo;s post or the documentation.
</p>
<p>
JTR&rsquo;s post is worth reading just for his dictionary advice but there&rsquo;s more.
</p>
<p>
Next he briefly discusses Hippie Expand, a way of completing words based on a list of functions that you can specify. I&rsquo;ve been using it for a long time, but his list of completion functions was more complete than mine so I stole it.
</p>
<p>
Finally, he considers ispell. If you&rsquo;ve been following along, you&rsquo;ll know that I recently started using <a href="https://github.com/minad/jinx">Jinx</a> instead of ispell but JTR showed me a couple of things I didn&rsquo;t know about. I&rsquo;ve always used ispell with flyspell so I never learned some of its extra tricks. One of those is a sort of fuzzy search for a word you&rsquo;re not sure how to spell. That happens to me a lot so I was glad to find out about ispell&rsquo;s ability to handle it.
</p>
<p>
The other thing you can do with ispell is to use it as a quick way of doing a replace. Take a look at JTR&rsquo;s post for more on both those functions.
</p>
<p>
JTR&rsquo;s post is from last week so Sacha will doubtless cover it before you see this but I had a lot to say about it so I decided to write about it anyway. It&rsquo;s definitely worth a careful reading.</p>
